-- title:
--   Fuchsian groups from rational division quaternion algebras have no cusps
-- statement:
--   Fix rational numbers $a,b$ and work in the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$. Assume $(hdiv)$ that every nonzero element of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ is a unit, i.e. that this algebra is a division algebra. Let $R$ be an arbitrary $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$, let $\iota \colon \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b] \to M_2(\mathbb{R})$ be a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra homomorphism, assumed injective, and let $c$ be a point of the one-point compactification $\mathrm{OnePoint}\ \mathbb{R} = \mathbb{R} \cup \{\infty\}$. Here `fuchsianGroup R ι` is the subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ obtained as the intersection of two subgroups: the image, under the map on unit groups induced by $\iota$, of `unitGroup R`, the subgroup of $(\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b])^\times$ generated by those units $u$ whose underlying element satisfies the predicate `IsUnitOf R`; and the kernel of the determinant homomorphism $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R}) \to \mathbb{R}^\times$. The conclusion is that $c$ is not a cusp of `fuchsianGroup R ι`, that is, there is no element of this group which is parabolic and fixes $c$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that the Fuchsian groups arising from unit groups in an indefinite rational quaternion division algebra are cusp-free, so that the associated quotient of the upper half-plane is a compact curve with no cusps, in contrast with the modular curves. It is used in the construction of the uniformised Hecke curve attached to such a Fuchsian group, [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_uniformizedHeckeCurve_fuchsianGroup`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_uniformizedHeckeCurve_fuchsianGroup).
